97
00:12:48,110 --> 00:12:48,576
As well?

98
00:12:48,910 --> 00:12:49,572
Good idea?

99
00:12:51,470 --> 00:12:53,216
Yeah, all of these are very good things.

100
00:12:53,456 --> 00:12:53,988
Last one.

101
00:12:56,014 --> 00:12:59,780
Yeah, also very good, but yeah, I I mean it it's a nasty question.

102
00:13:01,296 --> 00:13:05,616
In the sense that all of this is super important and all of these things which I named before.

103
00:13:05,776 --> 00:13:12,496
But the super important thing which is making software special is that you can make updates of software.

104
00:13:12,576 --> 00:13:15,332
You cannot update anything else, but you can update the software.

105
00:13:16,046 --> 00:13:22,976
So if there is any hardware failure, anything which is problematic with the spacecraft, the only thing you can do is update the software.

106
00:13:23,086 --> 00:13:24,676
There's basically nothing else you can do.

107
00:13:25,038 --> 00:13:27,936
You can change the way you are operating the spacecraft.

108
00:13:28,016 --> 00:13:34,532
You know, if there is a command which kills the software, for instance, then you say don't use that command or something like this.

109
00:13:35,182 --> 00:13:43,492
But the fact that you can update the software is extremely valuable because very often some hardware does not work as it should.

110
00:13:43,886 --> 00:13:50,416
For instance, the thermal limits are a little bit off or something like that, but you can change the software to change this.

111
00:13:50,510 --> 00:13:52,356
So it's an extremely important feature.

112
00:13:52,910 --> 00:13:59,012
Then another one which sounds a little bit silly, I would say, that it has no mass, you know, it's it does not wait.

113
00:13:59,246 --> 00:14:03,684
You know, it's uh you launch it and it uh does not add to the mass of the spacecraft.

114
00:14:04,334 --> 00:14:09,536
But it's not as silly because every kilogram of mass which you have to launch costs a lot of money.

115
00:14:09,616 --> 00:14:13,828
You need that launcher to bring the mass uh to space.

116
00:14:14,416 --> 00:14:21,604
And if you, for instance, upload new version of software which is doing some more stuff, it weighs the same.

117
00:14:21,742 --> 00:14:22,656
It's cool, right?

118
00:14:22,894 --> 00:14:24,964
It's uh it's not making any difference.

119
00:14:25,376 --> 00:14:33,572
Of course, if you need super like order of magnitude, better software, you might need bigger hardware or something like this, but that that's another matter.

120
00:14:35,536 --> 00:14:53,188
Um software also well cannot fail because of the things I mentioned, and um has to um adapt to all sorts of unforeseen events, and that's where I bring this example.

121
00:14:54,286 --> 00:14:59,716
it's our JUICE mission, which is flying as we speak to Jupiter.

122
00:15:00,014 --> 00:15:15,908
It was launched already in April 2023, and actually it started to be designed in 2012, just to give you some sort of uh you know perspective how long it takes to develop such a mission.

123
00:15:16,686 --> 00:15:20,612
It will arrive to Jupiter at uh in July 2031.

124
00:15:21,696 --> 00:15:23,652
So it's a really long time to get there.

125
00:15:24,336 --> 00:15:34,096
Um Jupiter is usually roughly half a billion kilometers from the Earth, between half a billion, that's the best case, and billion is the worst case, just to imagine.

126
00:15:34,318 --> 00:15:46,820
It does not fly there directly, instead, it's it it's sort of uh by a lot of flybys, it it sort of gets to an orbit of the sun to reach the orbit of Jupiter.

127
00:15:48,110 --> 00:16:01,508
So, and then mission, um another fact I should say um is the approximated cost of the mission is 1.7 billion euro for single spacecraft, including everything.

128
00:16:02,638 --> 00:16:14,340
Um so what happened there uh shortly after launch, uh, this big uh antenna, which you can see which goes uh uh vertically, was supposed to be opened and it got stuck.

129
00:16:14,862 --> 00:16:33,348
And the suspected cause of this was that a pin was stuck, you know, and then Then actions which were taken using software, it was possible to rotate the spacecraft, sort of to put sun on the pin to make it hot and then rotate again to make it cold.

130
00:16:33,582 --> 00:16:42,660
The difference between these two could be roughly 250 degrees, you know, like plus 100 degrees, minus 150 degrees, something like this.

131
00:16:43,182 --> 00:16:47,748
And it was believed that if you do this, then the pin will get unstuck.

132
00:16:48,366 --> 00:16:49,508
And this did not work.

133
00:16:49,776 --> 00:16:58,468
So instead, they started to prepare a sequence which was to do something with the thrusters, basically to shake the spacecraft or something like this.

134
00:16:59,278 --> 00:17:02,052
But in the end, they decided to do something different.

135
00:17:02,766 --> 00:17:11,140
Because the sequence was to open the antenna, sort of uh first the most outer elements, and then the next one, and the next one.

136
00:17:11,566 --> 00:17:22,256
And then the outer one did not open, and they decided to try to open the next one, and the shock caused the pin to unstuck and actually it deployed.

137
00:17:22,766 --> 00:17:30,916
So I guess what I wanted to demonstrate is that uh for a lot of these things, you know, this kind of flexibility is implemented by software.

138
00:17:31,310 --> 00:17:45,156
You know, the fact that you have the diagnostics, uh, for instance, the fact that the antenna did not deploy was seen by the control algorithm, which did not see the oscillations which were characteristic for the antenna being opened.

139
00:17:45,696 --> 00:17:48,388
So from there they realized that the antenna cannot be opened.

140
00:17:49,456 --> 00:17:53,540
I don't think there was a camera pointing into that direction, I'm not 100% sure.

141
00:17:54,126 --> 00:17:58,656
Then this kind of flexibility that you can uh rotate the spacecraft and things like that.

142
00:17:58,736 --> 00:18:09,936
You know, again, you have to have, of course, we have the experience to do this, so it's normal to have this kind of features on board, but you know, you have to prepare to be able to do this kind of stuff.

143
00:18:10,256 --> 00:18:20,420
Um, and yeah, the the flexibility of the control algorithm, for instance, you know, it needs to be designed with this kind of problems uh in mind.

144
00:18:21,550 --> 00:18:37,732
So I guess lessons learned uh software can support contingency situations, and then um another one it was important to have the model of the spacecraft on ground so that they were able to test this thing with the different sequence of folding the antenna.

145
00:18:40,046 --> 00:18:45,056
Another example I want to show, um and I have to speed up, I think.

146
00:18:45,216 --> 00:18:59,268
Um it's uh it was the Mars Express uh spacecraft, which was launched in December 2003, and it was planned for two years mission, and instead it kept operational, it's still operational right now.

147
00:18:59,790 --> 00:19:22,500
And around 2017, they realized that gyroscopes, so uh some uh units which are basically uh uh which which are used to determine uh like um uh like the direction of the spacecraft basically, um were basically projected to fail in two years.

148
00:19:23,374 --> 00:19:34,788
So what happened was that uh 15 years old code had to be hacked to change it to use Star Trekers for the navigation rather than the gyroscopes.

149
00:19:35,054 --> 00:19:42,372
These were used only occasionally, and that kind of code was uploaded one year later in April 2018.

150
00:19:43,056 --> 00:19:50,052
And um, and thanks to that, the gyroscopes work even now, and they are projected to work until 2034.

151
00:19:50,734 --> 00:19:59,844
So, so again, the the fact this demonstrates the fact that updating the software is super important because you can extend you know the lifetime of the mission.

152
00:20:00,976 --> 00:20:14,788
Um interestingly enough, the way we do this, but that's another matter, is not that you upload some application or something like that, but you upload the whole image to update the software.

153
00:20:15,136 --> 00:20:20,644
But uh yeah, I think it has something to do with the fact how we do this.

154
00:20:21,136 --> 00:20:33,136
So, this I wanted to show that there is a lot of, you know, this is called FlatSat, and it's basically a model of all the hardware of the what we call Avionics, which is on board a spacecraft.

155
00:20:33,216 --> 00:20:36,964
This one is a Metop second generation spacecraft made by Airbus.

156
00:20:37,856 --> 00:20:40,896
And and we use it to test the software.

157
00:20:41,056 --> 00:20:50,084
It's one of the steps to test the software because you cannot test everything on the spacecraft, because the spacecraft is used by other tests and other things, it's being integrated.

158
00:20:50,414 --> 00:20:53,060
It's not possible to do all the tests on the spacecraft.

159
00:20:53,582 --> 00:21:01,856
And there is software not only on the main onboard computer, but on the Star Trekers, on the GNSS receiver, on the payload computer.

160
00:21:01,936 --> 00:21:04,964
There are many computers with software on them.

161
00:21:06,958 --> 00:21:13,284
Now I got to the some of the challenges, like characteristics of spacecraft flight software.

162
00:21:14,702 --> 00:21:20,736
So I guess the most important one is that it's really embedded software, which is real-time software.

163
00:21:20,976 --> 00:21:37,572
Real time meaning not real fast, but real time meaning that the function, you know, the the fact that the software works is evaluated not only by the fact that it delivers the function, but also that it delivers it on time.

164
00:21:38,958 --> 00:21:40,964
Time is also an important aspect.

165
00:21:41,358 --> 00:22:06,336
Typically, how it works is that there are, I don't know, 30, 40 threads which constantly do something, some control algorithm with the frequency, I don't know, between 1 hertz, 10 hertz, 40 hertz, and every slot, you know, 20 milliseconds or so, there is like a deadline, and by that time the function needs to finish and make the computation.

166
00:22:06,510 --> 00:22:07,972
That's what your time software is.

167
00:22:08,878 --> 00:22:12,708
And then why this talk is called when less is more.

168
00:22:13,166 --> 00:22:24,484
You know, we work in a very harsh environment where uh there is strong radiation there, depending on which orbit you the spacecraft uh flies.

169
00:22:25,486 --> 00:22:36,100
And that means we have radiation-hardened hardware, and and which means that we have specialized hardware which is by far not as powerful as the hardware you are used to.

170
00:22:36,430 --> 00:22:43,556
So, to give you an example, typical spacecraft which fly today is something like 100 megahertz processors.

171
00:22:44,014 --> 00:22:55,972
The future spacecraft which are in preparation something like 250 MHz, quad core is our typical uh typical processor developed by Geissler in in Gothenburg.

172
00:22:56,718 --> 00:22:59,844
And we are working on one gigahertz processor, which would be octa core.

173
00:23:00,142 --> 00:23:02,532
So just to give you the idea.

174
00:23:03,822 --> 00:23:19,396
And then how we do this is uh, you know, the interesting thing is how we approach many of these challenges is not that we are super robust when it comes to architecture, architecture, architecture, you know, keep the architecture and it will work.

175
00:23:20,174 --> 00:23:21,476
It's more about the process.

176
00:23:21,696 --> 00:23:31,376
We have very strict standards which which mandate exactly which kind of documentation you you have to deliver, how you should code, you know, and this kind of stuff.

177
00:23:31,456 --> 00:23:33,892
I will get to that in more detail.

178
00:23:35,342 --> 00:23:42,148
Um safety and reliability, obviously, these things have to work reliably, so that's another challenge.

179
00:23:42,496 --> 00:23:47,376
Um and again, I'm mentioning there independent software verification and validation.

180
00:23:47,470 --> 00:23:49,668
So again, the focus is on the process.

181
00:23:50,382 --> 00:24:00,096
And independent verification and validation means that we for critical software, not we, but in fact, we don't develop this the spacecraft.

182
00:24:00,176 --> 00:24:06,276
We have suppliers who develop the spacecraft for us, and they have other suppliers who develop the software.

183
00:24:07,182 --> 00:24:13,056
And they also have to hire third party which is checking the software independently.

184
00:24:13,294 --> 00:24:24,964
So it's additional cost, you know, that you have someone else who is checking your software and verifying the requirements, verifying the design, verifying the code, and running independent tests as well.

185
00:24:27,022 --> 00:24:29,348
Another challenge we have is complexity.

186
00:24:31,056 --> 00:24:39,076
although we are not talking about millions of lines of code, but more hundreds of thousands of lines of code, something like this.

187
00:24:39,854 --> 00:24:45,776
But it's also the complexity given that it's real-time software and very tightly coupled and things like that.

188
00:24:46,062 --> 00:24:55,844
So we use software frameworks, we use advanced verification validation techniques, we use a little bit of model based software engineering.

189
00:24:57,454 --> 00:24:59,456
System integration is another challenge.

190
00:24:59,616 --> 00:25:08,036
We use uh software which is provided by multiple parties, and again, because of this tight coupling and timing, the integration is quite tricky.

191
00:25:08,976 --> 00:25:20,900
Um Also, what is a little bit tricky, but I will get there is when your software runs on hardware and the hardware is usually delivered late or is specified late, so that's another problem.

192
00:25:21,614 --> 00:25:23,972
And then configuration management and updates.

193
00:25:24,398 --> 00:25:26,788
Some of the software is used on multiple satellites.

194
00:25:27,552 --> 00:25:32,132
There are late changes, updates in flight, and we have to manage all that.

195
00:25:33,152 --> 00:25:37,348
And this becomes more complex for large spacecraft constellations.

196
00:25:39,598 --> 00:26:01,540
Basically, we try to move from like text-based specifications and some drawings, manual coding, testing, towards more executable specifications, automatic documentation, ultimately bug-free software, using model-based software engineering, state machines, model checking.

197
00:26:02,032 --> 00:26:08,192
But what I want to say is you know, this is more that's where we are going, let's say.

198
00:26:12,912 --> 00:26:22,404
For instance, for attitudinal orbit control, we use uh MATLAB, you know, and then from there we generate the code, and that that's working.

199
00:26:23,246 --> 00:26:26,192
But there is one but which is important.

200
00:26:26,512 --> 00:26:38,212
We don't trust the generated code, which means that this kind of mantra or you do while you are doing model-based uh software engineering is to have correctness by design.

201
00:26:38,992 --> 00:26:48,512
And we because we don't trust the code generator so much, we anyway test the generated code the same way we test handwritten code.

202
00:26:48,672 --> 00:26:49,732
There's no difference for us.

203
00:26:52,112 --> 00:27:01,312
Um there is a little bit of game change when it comes to uh large constellations, like you know, probably Starlink, 6,000 satellites.

204
00:27:01,472 --> 00:27:05,092
Ourselves, I mentioned we work on Iris Square, 290 satellites.

205
00:27:05,710 --> 00:27:07,300
There are some other constellations.

206
00:27:07,886 --> 00:27:16,356
We used we you know, we were used to one-off satellites, which were like the JUICE one I presented before, which is sort of one-off mission.

207
00:27:16,654 --> 00:27:22,628
There are some product lines and reused libraries and this and that, but but it's one-off satellite.

208
00:27:23,342 --> 00:27:45,792
And and we have more and more these kinds of constellations where we need to have a little bit different mindset, you know, which means it's a bit of serial production, even of the chips, you know, it's not like that you have the target hardened, radiation hardened processor and several models which are not radiation hardened, where you test the flight software, but instead you have a little bit different scale.

209
00:27:45,872 --> 00:27:54,788
Because if you are launching 200 satellites, then uh you know you need production of 200 flight models and some number of the test models, etc.

210
00:27:55,566 --> 00:28:05,552
So there is definitely there are challenges linked to that when it comes to autonomy, because again, we cannot operate the satellites the same way if there is 200 of them.

211
00:28:05,902 --> 00:28:12,836
So it cannot be that there is 200 people talking the old style to the satellite, you know, it's not working like this.

212
00:28:13,230 --> 00:28:15,972
So that's another challenge which is relatively recent.

213
00:28:16,878 --> 00:28:24,548
Obviously, the challenge to do with configuration management with all those satellites and how we do operations, how we do maintenance.

214
00:28:26,670 --> 00:28:33,636
Very often software ends up being on a critical path because it relies on the design of the hardware.

215
00:28:34,032 --> 00:28:44,592
And very often that comes late, or there are changes, or or we get a simulator which is not uh really accurate, and therefore we need to change the software.

216
00:28:44,878 --> 00:28:52,112
So, what happens is that software is often blamed to be late and delaying the mission, but in reality it's more complicated.

217
00:28:52,272 --> 00:29:02,372
Software is the last thing which you can change to make everything work, so it's sometimes blamed uh not for no good reason, but says flight software engineer.

218
00:29:03,072 --> 00:29:15,236
Um but um but overall it's believed that the complexity, size, and verification of flight software are underestimated and causing troubles.

219
00:29:15,790 --> 00:29:22,512
And then also worldwide, there are some uh examples of big failures which are caused by software.

220
00:29:22,672 --> 00:29:27,312
Some of them I mentioned there, be it NASA or Japanese Space Agency or ESA.

221
00:29:28,462 --> 00:29:35,104
And I am going to talk about one of them which is relatively famous in my domain.

222
00:29:35,264 --> 00:29:38,276
I'm not sure all of you know this one.

223
00:29:38,624 --> 00:29:42,596
And it's the Ariane 5 maiden flight on 4th June 1996.

224
00:29:43,086 --> 00:29:51,844
And what happened there was that Ariane 5 reused part of the software from Ariane 4.

225
00:29:52,224 --> 00:29:56,784
And then the behavior of Ariane 4 is slightly different.

226
00:29:56,878 --> 00:30:04,900
And then there was a variable called horizontal bias, was basically overflow.

227
00:30:12,558 --> 00:30:13,956
And there was an overflow.

228
00:30:15,104 --> 00:30:17,504
There are several other facts related to this.

229
00:30:17,744 --> 00:30:19,972
One, why was it not checked?

230
00:30:20,624 --> 00:30:23,844
Back then, and that's not completely software issue.

231
00:30:29,264 --> 00:30:36,304
But the requirement was to use only 80% of that of the CPU on that processor.

232
00:30:36,558 --> 00:30:48,932
And it was not possible in the ADA language which was used back then to have the overflow checked on all the variables which were in the system because that basically increases the CPU load.

233
00:30:50,624 --> 00:30:55,780
So four of the variables were checked, three of them were analyzed to be okay.

234
00:30:56,110 --> 00:30:59,684
And this one was analyzed but on Ariane 4, not on Ariane 5.

235
00:31:00,334 --> 00:31:04,784
The other thing is that the software which failed was actually not used.

236
00:31:04,864 --> 00:31:10,692
It was only supposed to operate until the launch minus nine seconds.

237
00:31:11,344 --> 00:31:14,180
So the software did not need to operate.

238
00:31:57,344 --> 00:32:12,868
How you should reuse that kind of software, you know, like with some sort of set of preconditions, postconditions, some way of formalizing the constraints, how you can use that software.

239
00:32:13,198 --> 00:32:14,372
And this was not done.

240
00:32:14,798 --> 00:32:19,824
The failure would not have happened if the building block design contract was documented and checked.

241
00:32:20,944 --> 00:32:32,868
And the other way around, of course, um it's important to validate the envelope in which some software is running, to see what is the what are the conditions, what is the execution environment.

242
00:32:33,422 --> 00:32:34,436
So this did not happen.

243
00:32:35,182 --> 00:32:39,620
And then there are some other lessons learned from this.

244
00:32:40,430 --> 00:32:52,384
To test all realistic scenarios, use end-to-end simulation, uh, do uh fault injection, robustness and stress testing, uh, this kind of stuff.

245
00:32:52,544 --> 00:33:19,012
But also, when it comes to software architecture, um, things like, uh, if the system design was in such a way, is done but in such a way that one process does not kill the whole software on the spacecraft or on that processor, but uh, because it was single space or single address space operating system, it basically killed the whole processing on that computer.

246
00:33:19,502 --> 00:33:33,284
Another one is to have different kinds of redundancy concept because in fact there was a redundant computer there, but it was running exactly the same software, so that software failed as well, you know, like one second later or something like this.

247
00:33:33,870 --> 00:33:37,344
But you can have concept which we call dissimilar software.

248
00:33:37,582 --> 00:33:42,564
So basically, to have another supplier develop the same software again and but differently.

249
00:33:43,406 --> 00:33:45,504
But obviously, that's not for free, you know.

250
00:33:45,584 --> 00:33:48,132
So um, but it's it's an option.

251
00:33:49,038 --> 00:33:56,516
And then um, I guess uh yeah, if there was some sort of intelligent exception handling, this would this would help as well.

252
00:33:57,262 --> 00:34:07,744
And then one of the one of the lessons learned led also to creating this uh independent verification and validation process, which is at ESA.

253
00:34:09,104 --> 00:34:20,944
So now I have some sort of um set of uh best practices, what we do, um uh you know how we work with flight software.

254
00:34:21,024 --> 00:34:27,824
I I will try to go through it quite quickly because I can see that time is uh flying and the next session is right after.

255
00:34:28,944 --> 00:34:39,044
So basically, really uh the the the the most important thing how we tackle this kind of critical software is to follow very strict engineering process.

256
00:34:39,824 --> 00:34:47,204
That's that's how we approach it, and we tailor it based on software criticality, and I will talk a little bit about the software criticality later.

257
00:34:47,854 --> 00:34:59,524
We are we are strictly driven by requirements, and we have traces from requirements through design to code and tests which are testing each of the lines of the code and requirements.

258
00:34:59,904 --> 00:35:04,516
We have full traceability between all of these things.

259
00:35:05,134 --> 00:35:14,308
We have many types of tests, starting with tests at software level only, uh, which is uh maybe in some simulated environment, that's okay.

260
00:35:14,606 --> 00:35:29,984
But then we validate every requirement on realistic hardware, and then we have some other what we call system level tests, where we integrate with the rest of the spacecraft, we have functional tests, we have what we call operations tests.

261
00:35:30,064 --> 00:35:43,428
Like if you imagine that Earth orbiter flies once per 100 minutes around the Earth and twice per orbit is dumping the data to ground station and it's scanning on the sunny side for 40 minutes.

262
00:35:43,904 --> 00:35:53,584
So there is a test which is mimicking exactly this kind of stuff, which is happening every 100 minutes, is always the same, just to see that the software can cope with it.

263
00:35:53,664 --> 00:35:56,304
There are no buffer overruns or something like this.

264
00:35:56,814 --> 00:36:11,140
It's important to have realistic data to test the software, and then we we do something called schedulability analysis, so some mathematical analysis to show that the real-time properties of the software are met.

265
00:36:11,886 --> 00:36:14,384
Our mantra is to keep the software simple.

266
00:36:14,544 --> 00:36:22,544
That's again back to when uh the title of the presentation, which is when uh less is more.

267
00:36:23,104 --> 00:36:28,304
Um, you know, we really implement only the essential uh capability.

268
00:36:28,878 --> 00:36:48,544
To give you an example with fold management, you know, often it's not a good idea that the software would try to analyze what precisely happened and having some complicated logic to nail it down to ah, actually, this variable is wrong, so you know I go to this execution path and that and that yada.

269
00:36:48,944 --> 00:36:50,692
It's easier to say, oh, it failed.

270
00:36:50,990 --> 00:36:52,784
Okay, let's just reboot and that's it.

271
00:36:52,864 --> 00:36:56,144
You know, and you save many, many lines of code.

272
00:36:56,224 --> 00:37:05,604
Uh, if you can afford it to handle the failure this way, then it's better than to be too smart and add many, many lines of code to implement this.

273
00:37:06,766 --> 00:37:12,772
We don't we ban having any unused code or uh deactivated code.

274
00:37:13,742 --> 00:37:17,284
And another mantra is test as you fly, fly as you test.

275
00:37:17,934 --> 00:37:20,150
I guess it's self-descripting.

276
00:37:21,582 --> 00:37:23,424
And then other good practices we have.

277
00:37:23,504 --> 00:37:31,332
We have coding standards, defensive programming, we gather code quality metrics of all sorts.

278
00:37:31,584 --> 00:37:40,132
We we have design margins, for instance, at preliminary design review, we need to have 50% of memory free, 50% of CPU not used.

279
00:37:40,686 --> 00:37:49,700
At launch, it's 25% of memory not used, and 25% of CPU not used, just in case of having to update the software later.

280
00:37:50,574 --> 00:37:56,580
Um we do all sorts of analysis, code inspections, um, all sorts of things.

281
00:37:57,584 --> 00:38:01,668
Um also I mentioned that we define criticality of software.

282
00:38:02,736 --> 00:38:10,628
And that's defined, it's not just some guesswork, it's defined based on the consequence.

283
00:38:10,990 --> 00:38:13,924
You know, what is the severity of the failure?

284
00:38:14,416 --> 00:38:28,896
So, category A software is like for human spacecraft, uh, or in case there is the the failure would cause some uh big catastrophe, you know, like having some damage on the ground or something like that.

285
00:38:29,136 --> 00:38:32,420
That's on the that's the one end, which is criticality A.

286
00:38:32,896 --> 00:38:37,668
And on the other hand, criticality D is basically ground software.

287
00:38:38,510 --> 00:38:41,380
And then uh B and C is somewhere in between.

288
00:38:41,936 --> 00:39:07,460
And related to it is cost, because um, you know, we have uh strict requirements when it comes to code coverage by tests, which is 100% um, decision coverage and statement coverage for class criticality A and B, and MCDC coverage, which is basically checking every condition, every boolean variable which is impacting decision, uh, needs to be verified.

289
00:39:07,822 --> 00:39:10,884
So that's additional test cases, and that's for class A.

290
00:39:11,342 --> 00:39:20,676
And on top of it, um also coverage of object code by tests in case there is no clear traceability between source code and object code.

291
00:39:23,150 --> 00:39:30,628
Um when it comes to AI, and I got to that point, so there are two ways how we use AI.

292
00:39:31,056 --> 00:39:34,436
Um one is for the production of software.

293
00:39:35,118 --> 00:39:38,180
We don't do any vibe coding or anything like this.

294
00:39:39,216 --> 00:39:54,756
Um basically, because we have all of these super strict uh procedures, standards, we anyway need to have full traceability with the design and with the tests and having full trace how it is all working and tested and verified.

295
00:39:55,296 --> 00:39:59,140
We just don't see that it would help us that we generate some code.

296
00:40:00,078 --> 00:40:12,484
But obviously, I can see long term that it is possible that you take the standard and you ask the LLM, like, okay, you have to do it this way, you need to generate the documentation for me for the humans to verify it.

297
00:40:12,816 --> 00:40:19,056
But right now we don't do it because it looks like um too complicated and we don't trust it basically.

298
00:40:20,110 --> 00:40:25,348
I mean, this business is quite conservative, so we we have to be careful there.

299
00:40:26,896 --> 00:40:43,844
Um otherwise, yeah, we we use uh AI for all sorts of verifications to verify consistency of requirements, all this traceability, finding code defects, and defining even test suits and things like that.

300
00:40:44,206 --> 00:40:48,644
And for that, it has been used and it's quite successful, I have to say.

301
00:40:50,670 --> 00:41:02,176
Um when it comes to flying AI on board, um uh we are limited once again by the environment in which we are flying, uh, in two ways.

302
00:41:02,336 --> 00:41:13,776
One, that the GPUs which you use in computers for AI are not radiation hardened, but we are experimenting with some software which is actually more powerful and radiation hardened.

303
00:41:14,222 --> 00:41:21,536
But another one is that um these processors need a significant uh amount of power, and that's another constraint.

304
00:41:21,616 --> 00:41:23,296
You know, we cannot afford flying.

305
00:41:23,598 --> 00:41:31,456
We typically we generate power by solar arrays, and we cannot afford to have hundred square meters of solar arrays just because we fly AI.

306
00:41:31,536 --> 00:41:32,740
But we are getting there.

307
00:41:34,478 --> 00:41:52,452
Example applications where we can use AI on board is like image processing, you know, basically to drop some images which are detected to be bad, unusable, which can help saving the bandwidth because the bandwidth to communicate the data back to Earth is limited, obviously.

308
00:41:53,134 --> 00:41:59,248
Then things like AI-based mission planning, you know, this would be a nice feature.

309
00:41:59,408 --> 00:42:00,996
Today it's always done by humans.

310
00:42:01,838 --> 00:42:10,468
Some of the control algorithms could be assisted by AI, like proximity operations and things like that, you know, like docking operations.

311
00:42:10,958 --> 00:42:13,008
I can imagine that we can use AI.

312
00:42:13,168 --> 00:42:15,300
We don't do it, but we are experimenting with it.

313
00:42:15,662 --> 00:42:19,492
Visual navigation is another one, and onboard fault management.

314
00:42:20,048 --> 00:42:34,852
And especially it would be I'm pretty sure that it will be possible to detect, no, to forecast failures based on the data which is collected, and to forecast the failures on ground or directly on board.

315
00:42:35,662 --> 00:42:40,580
But again, we are now experimenting with it, not that it's working.

316
00:42:40,942 --> 00:42:44,448
There are some challenges to fly AI.

317
00:42:44,928 --> 00:42:46,848
Um data quality.

318
00:42:47,008 --> 00:42:52,528
There is noise in space in the sense that there are all sorts of particles, you know.

319
00:42:52,608 --> 00:43:05,636
So you basically you train on some data set uh on the ground, and then in space it could be different because there are some, you know, because of the radiation in the data, there are all sorts of bit flips and things like that.

320
00:43:05,902 --> 00:43:08,848
And then there are physical obstacles and particles.

321
00:43:08,928 --> 00:43:15,332
So the data looks different, so it can happen that the model actually does not work because you trained on different data sets.

322
00:43:15,568 --> 00:43:16,772
So that's a tricky part.

323
00:43:17,134 --> 00:43:22,148
Robustness, but that's the usual um problem with uh AI.

324
00:43:22,542 --> 00:43:26,852
Verifiability, maintainability, and I mentioned the computational power.

325
00:43:28,128 --> 00:43:31,808
Um I I think I need to a little bit speed up.

326
00:43:31,888 --> 00:43:36,388
I wanted to mention this uh thing because it's about long-term maintenance.

327
00:43:36,910 --> 00:43:42,928
I already mentioned the spacecraft called Mars Orbiter, where it was important that you can upload, update the software.

328
00:43:43,248 --> 00:43:49,488
But I just wanted to emphasize that sometimes, you know, you first of all you have to plan this.

329
00:43:49,648 --> 00:43:53,540
You have to plan that the software might be updated in the future.

330
00:43:53,728 --> 00:44:14,528
So you need to document, you know, to keep all the documentation, including the build process, you know, the documenting the build process, keeping all the tools, like keeping GCC version from 20 years ago, just in case that you will need to recompile the software, you know.

331
00:44:14,862 --> 00:44:21,248
So it's it's not a trivial thing that you keep for a long time all your uh or your.

332
00:44:21,774 --> 00:44:24,768
I see that you are smiling because you had a similar problem, right?

333
00:44:24,848 --> 00:44:26,528
Uh that was presented yesterday.

334
00:44:26,926 --> 00:44:45,284
Yeah, and and I wanted to just to point at this uh this gentleman, uh gentleman who works for NASA JPL, David Cummings, he had a very nice talk at the flight software workshop uh last year, which is called How We Diagnosed and Fixed 2023 Voyager 1 Anomaly from 15 billion miles away.

335
00:44:45,742 --> 00:44:53,156
So basically, what happened there 47 years after launch, uh Voyager 1 stopped communicating or was communicating some noise.

336
00:44:53,710 --> 00:44:57,572
And then this gentleman with a team of guys uh were supposed to fix it.

337
00:44:58,158 --> 00:45:01,220
To send a command there takes over 22 hours.

338
00:45:01,614 --> 00:45:04,836
To get something back, it's over 22 hours, obviously, as well.

339
00:45:05,358 --> 00:45:07,396
It was documented on printed materials.

340
00:45:07,968 --> 00:45:09,648
Uh he had to figure it out.

341
00:45:09,808 --> 00:45:13,408
He did not have any assembler, any simulator, any test bed.

342
00:45:13,966 --> 00:45:15,556
He did not have the source code.

343
00:45:15,886 --> 00:45:22,948
So, in fact, he worked on the machine code and he was able to really to debug on paper.

344
00:45:23,406 --> 00:45:26,948
And I really recommend you to watch uh watch this presentation.

345
00:45:27,278 --> 00:45:37,088
Uh at the end of the presentation, one of the questions from the audience was uh David, can you tell us who is your favorite actor to act yourself in a future movie?

346
00:45:37,248 --> 00:45:40,528
Because it was really, really amazing piece of work.

347
00:45:43,182 --> 00:45:45,360
There's something similar with IOLU spacecraft.

348
00:45:45,440 --> 00:45:50,980
I will skip this, and I will skip mostly also this one just for the sake of saving time.

349
00:45:53,198 --> 00:46:02,308
Another failure of our proba mission this earlier this year, and basically there was something like deadlock, you know, wrong use of mutexes.

350
00:46:03,566 --> 00:46:08,708
And it's like you can find how to do it properly in every textbook, but still it happens.

351
00:46:08,942 --> 00:46:14,468
You know, it's very complex code with many threads, many execution paths, many options.

352
00:46:14,880 --> 00:46:19,360
What is the sequence of using some shared resources and things like that?

353
00:46:19,630 --> 00:46:21,828
So it can still happen, and it did happen.

354
00:46:22,400 --> 00:46:44,228
So, I guess lessons learned from this are maybe if you had better software architecture, some sort of framework, so that you are not using mutexes, actually, you are using some high-level primitives, and maybe you know you are not fiddling with the mutexes, because otherwise you have to analyze it, and still it can happen that because of this, you might lose a spacecraft.

355
00:46:46,350 --> 00:46:51,120
Um and that's uh the next step towards a flight software framework.

356
00:46:51,280 --> 00:47:05,840
So between 2008 and now, we have been trying to have some sort of flight software framework, and we proposed one, we have one, which is based on some sort of execution platform and standard services on top of it.

357
00:47:06,158 --> 00:47:17,316
You know, the execution platform contains operating system and some sort of middleware, and then you have some building blocks on top of it, some components which have provided and required interfaces and something like that.

358
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:20,772
Um why this is good?

359
00:47:21,200 --> 00:47:28,000
Because it makes us focus on mission-specific applications and not on something we fly every single time.

360
00:47:28,080 --> 00:47:31,460
It's we always fly the same infrastructure, roughly.

361
00:47:31,854 --> 00:47:38,276
So it would it enables reuse across missions, improves quality, reliability.

362
00:47:39,886 --> 00:47:45,476
Our problem is that we have not managed to use it beyond just reference architecture.

363
00:47:45,838 --> 00:47:57,440
So, how we use it is that at least with our industry, because we have different suppliers and they've developed their own frameworks, so at least we use the same language so we understand each other, but that's it basically.

364
00:47:57,600 --> 00:48:03,204
We don't have like a unified framework which uh everyone would be using in our business.

365
00:48:04,174 --> 00:48:09,280
We tried to standardize interfaces and we failed because that was not possible.

366
00:48:09,550 --> 00:48:19,396
And instead, we started to think about at least standardizing how we specify interfaces, you know, having some sort of formal specification of data interfaces.

367
00:48:19,982 --> 00:48:22,340
And that's something we call electronic data sheets.

368
00:48:23,200 --> 00:48:40,324
Um, I wanted to talk about it because there is there was like an anomaly of Sentinel-2, where basically you know something called Star Treker was misaligned because of wrong interpretation that the matrix should be transposed or normalized.

369
00:48:40,590 --> 00:48:49,680
And we took special care for the next release of Sentinel to be, I was part of the team, so I I know very uh I know a lot about this.

370
00:48:49,966 --> 00:49:00,356
And and despite all the effort, still next time around, we made the same mistake that some matrix was actually wrongly understood not to be transposed, and again there was some misalignment.

371
00:49:00,718 --> 00:49:25,920
So, my point is, um, if we used some formal specification, and you can imagine that it's tricky to use formal description of a matrix and sort of give it a semantics to say how you should use it, whether it should be transposed or whether it should be normalized, because it sounds like, um, plain English, you know, it's difficult to find specification language to do this.

372
00:49:26,222 --> 00:49:29,220
But if we were able to do this, we would avoid this kind of problems.

373
00:49:29,680 --> 00:49:44,932
So basically, you know, we we really hope to do something like this that from what we call electronic data sheet, we would be able to generate flight software, integration tests, simulation models, human readable description of interfaces.

374
00:49:45,280 --> 00:49:52,420
But we still keep working on this because it's difficult to make agreement across European industry.

375
00:49:55,790 --> 00:50:12,580
Future trends, as I said, large constellations and like industrial production of satellites, we will use more commercial off-the-shelf hardware, and then the software will be more complex because we will have to deal with failures because of radiation.

376
00:50:13,742 --> 00:50:22,628
Use of multi-cores, hypervisors, which will allow us to have systems which have different criticality on the same processor.

377
00:50:23,438 --> 00:50:34,148
And as I said, advanced autonomy, onboard data processing, AI-assisted end-to-end flight software development, more models.

378
00:50:35,022 --> 00:50:40,004
And two important things, and I in some conversations I discussed it with some of you.

379
00:50:40,302 --> 00:50:45,604
And one of our strategic goals is actually to have European flight software framework.

380
00:50:46,848 --> 00:50:51,684
And I think that's your domain, so I would be happy to hear what you think about it.

381
00:50:52,048 --> 00:50:56,768
But really, it could be the European flight software framework could be a lot of things.

382
00:51:21,198 --> 00:51:35,568
What it is about is basically to fly fewer number of processors and sort of by updating applications on those processors to change the function of the spacecraft.

383
00:51:35,694 --> 00:51:36,836
It's similar in cars.

384
00:51:37,102 --> 00:51:47,648
They would have a tendency that you have the same box on lower class Mercedes and better class Mercedes, but by updating the software, you actually have better.

385
00:51:47,822 --> 00:51:56,196
I don't know what one of the systems can be a better version because it's better software, but otherwise the processor is the same.

386
00:51:57,582 --> 00:52:01,568
Finally, I was literally I'm I'm sweat.

387
00:52:01,728 --> 00:52:04,368
I had to push uh all of the material through.

388
00:52:04,528 --> 00:52:11,812
Um so what I wanted to say uh to repeat myself flight software is critical.

389
00:52:12,448 --> 00:52:15,748
Uh spacecraft is as good as the flight software.

390
00:52:16,528 --> 00:52:24,356
Um it's it's common to underestimate the resources needed to do proper flight software.

391
00:52:25,358 --> 00:52:33,124
As I said, the way we see flight software is that we strongly focus on the development process.

392
00:52:34,222 --> 00:52:43,648
Things like software architecture, etc., maybe it comes from that, that you you have good software architecture to be able to follow the process well.

393
00:52:43,808 --> 00:52:45,088
But that's not our focus.

394
00:52:45,248 --> 00:52:50,468
Our standards don't say how you should do your flight software architecture.

395
00:52:52,238 --> 00:52:55,268
Obviously, it helps a lot when you use modern tools.

396
00:52:55,950 --> 00:53:01,636
But anyway, proper software architecture and using software frameworks, we see it as a key thing.

397
00:53:02,288 --> 00:53:08,388
Um makes reuse easier and it helps you avoid common errors.

398
00:53:08,622 --> 00:53:10,308
Some of those I showed to you.

399
00:53:10,958 --> 00:53:17,124
The software code which is behind all of these frameworks is as complex as it always has been.

400
00:53:17,486 --> 00:53:31,808
You know, when you get to the point that you need to fix a machine code because something is failing, you still have to understand all the details how it's you know how the machine code uh really works.

401
00:53:32,128 --> 00:53:35,648
But if you can avoid it at least during the development, it's good.

402
00:53:35,886 --> 00:53:45,888
But then there is, I guess, the tricky question yeah, if you let AI do everything for you, who will be the expert to patch the code like David Cummings did with the Voyager 1?

403
00:53:46,510 --> 00:53:54,404
And then finally, yeah, I mean, some of it, what I presented are lessons learned, showing freely mistakes we made.

404
00:53:54,638 --> 00:53:56,132
I strongly believe in this.

405
00:53:56,750 --> 00:53:58,660
Don't be ashamed of making mistakes.

406
00:53:59,168 --> 00:54:02,244
Uh face it, share it, learn from it.

407
00:54:03,054 --> 00:54:06,848
Talk to people around because many of them know more than you do.

408
00:54:07,342 --> 00:54:12,548
And as the classic says, a mistake repeated more than once is not a mistake, but it's a choice.

409
00:54:13,198 --> 00:54:15,248
So by that I finish.

410
00:54:15,408 --> 00:54:17,488
Thanks very much for your attention.

